{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfijeQfEnM8wT5xjTVKc6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've written the round-2 exploratory candidate, `personal_second_order_chance_typicality`, and it passes the admission self-check. It probably fits worse than the current best model, though.\n\n**The hypothesis:** this is a different kind of process from the current set. Every model there scores a sequence by how much better a fair coin explains it than some \"regular\" generator does. This one has no rival explanation. A sequence looks random to the extent that the person's own imagined fair coin would readily produce it. That imagined coin is second-order: each person has their own belief about how likely chance is to switch right after a repeat, and how likely right after a switch. So someone who thinks chance rarely switches twice in a row will see long perfect alternation as non-random, and someone who thinks repeats quickly give way to switches will see streaks as non-random.\n\n**How it's built:** the features are differences in transition counts (switch or repeat after a repeat, switch or repeat after a switch, plus the first transition) and the sequence length. The two switch beliefs vary by person (non-centred, 400 slots so new participants can be predicted). Decisiveness is shared, and the evidence is weighed per flip by a fitted power of length.\n\n**Checks:**\n- **Self-check:** prints `OK` — it loads, the log-probability is finite, the data contract holds, and ELPD-LOO is finite. Its smoke-fit ELPD-LOO of −2573.8 is well below the best model's −2105.7, so it is likely to rank low. That short fit also warned that PSIS-LOO looked unreliable (4.6% of trials with Pareto k > 0.5); a single 100-draw chain may simply be too short to judge this.\n- **Speed:** the smoke check took about 11 minutes, so I timed sampling separately. One chain of 500 tuning and 500 draw iterations took 171 s, with no divergences and bulk ESS between 174 and 443 for the main parameters. That should finish well inside the 30-minute admission limit, but convergence (R-hat ≤ 1.05 across chains) is only tested in admission's full fit.\n- **Fitted beliefs:** after a repeat, people expect a switch with probability about 0.99; after a switch, only about 0.23. Both vary a lot between people.\n\nThe critique's other findings are not addressed: the slight preference for tail-heavy sequences, and the left/right habits (side-bias spread and perseveration). Exploratory slots can't borrow components from other models, and each needs a cue beyond this one mechanism.\n\nFiles are in `experiment2/model_loop/iter_2/candidate_1/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":63504,"cache_read_input_tokens":10102,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":63504,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4069b6cd-77f7-43ec-8f0b-09442a7306af","uuid":"e7ac8f5a-2202-4855-b7c5-a23994447c35","timestamp":"2026-10-05T07:59:13.448Z","request_id":"req_011CfijePR5xFsb45SakEV81"}
